1
00:00:08,620 --> 00:00:10,690
It's from Game of Thrones.

2
00:00:10,690 --> 00:00:11,690
What do you think?

3
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:13,500
I don't know.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:16,210
If we're going to start a fantasy sword collection--

5
00:00:16,210 --> 00:00:18,470
and I've long thought we should--

6
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:21,860
is this really the sword to start with?

7
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:23,470
What did you have in mind?

8
00:00:23,470 --> 00:00:27,050
Well, off the top of my head, I'd have to go with Excalibur.

9
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:30,570
It gives you the right to rule England.

10
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:34,060
It would be a replica of a movie prop.

11
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:35,690
Fair enough.

12
00:00:35,690 --> 00:00:38,940
It'd give you the right to rule a replica of England.

13
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:41,900
Well, they don't have an Excalibur here,

14
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:43,320
so what do you want to do?

15
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:45,900
Mm. Tough decision.

16
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:47,790
There's no weaponry from Lord of the Rings

17
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:50,520
forged in a Chinese sweatshop?

18
00:00:52,310 --> 00:00:55,030
Just Bilbo Baggins' sword over there.

19
00:00:55,030 --> 00:00:58,470
Two grown men with a hobbit's dagger; wouldn't we look silly?

20
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,050
Okay, let's go for it.

21
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:05,710
Oh, I see you guys have found my little treasure.

22
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:07,300
Yeah.

23
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:08,470
It's okay, I guess.

24
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:10,200
Okay? It's magnificent.

25
00:01:13,730 --> 00:01:15,110
What do you want for it?

26
00:01:15,110 --> 00:01:17,540
Oh, it's hard to put a price on something

27
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:20,430
that's a copy of something that was on pay cable.

28
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:23,600
But for my friends, let's say 250?

29
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:25,710
Oh, that's pretty steep.

30
00:01:25,710 --> 00:01:26,830
Well, it's a limited edition.

31
00:01:26,830 --> 00:01:29,910
They only made 8,000 of these bad boys.

32
00:01:30,410 --> 00:01:31,640
Only 8,000?

33
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:33,860
We're wasting precious time. Buy it.

34
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:36,310
Hang on.

35
00:01:36,310 --> 00:01:37,550
Can you do any better?

36
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:38,370
Are you kidding?

37
00:01:38,370 --> 00:01:40,660
I'm already giving you the friends and family discount.

38
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:41,560
Oh, did you hear that?

39
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:43,270
We're getting the friends and family discount.

40
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:45,780
We are honored and we will take it.

41
00:01:45,780 --> 00:01:47,670
Slow down.

42
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:48,440
Two hundred.

43
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:49,300
What are you doing?

44
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:50,800
250 is already the discounted price.

45
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:52,110
Will you shut up?

46
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:54,130
Tell you what, I'll go 235.

47
00:01:54,130 --> 00:01:55,520
Nope. Maybe another time.

48
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:57,590
Okay, 225-- my final offer.

49
00:01:57,590 --> 00:01:59,270
Take it, take it.

50
00:02:01,270 --> 00:02:02,380
Two hundred.

51
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:03,630
Man, you're killing me!

52
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:05,620
Killing you? I can't breathe.

53
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:09,240
210, and I'm losing money.

54
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:12,290
Oh, now, we can't let him lose money, Leonard. I'm so sorry.

55
00:02:12,830 --> 00:02:15,410
210 and you throw in the Iron Man helmet.

56
00:02:15,410 --> 00:02:16,100
Are you crazy?

57
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:18,180
That helmet's signed by Robert Downey Jr.

58
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:19,770
So?

59
00:02:19,770 --> 00:02:21,010
Okay, if you're going to question

60
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:23,510
the importance of an actor's signature on a plastic helmet

61
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:25,450
from a movie based on a comic book,

62
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:28,570
then all of our lives have no meaning!

63
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:33,780
Okay, fine.

64
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:35,720
Just the sword, 210.

65
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:36,920
Thank you.

66
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:38,900
I can eat meat this week.

67
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:40,920
See that?

68
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:42,540
I just saved us 40 bucks.

69
00:02:42,540 --> 00:02:44,600
I've long said, what you lack in academic knowledge

70
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,660
you make up for in street smarts.

71
00:02:47,550 --> 00:02:48,620
You want me to wrap it?

72
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:49,420
No, it's okay.

73
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:51,920
I'm gonna stab my friend in the chest.

74
00:02:53,490 --> 00:02:54,540
Hey, Stuart.

75
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:55,800
Ah, hey, Wil.

76
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:57,850
Hello, Wil Wheaton.

77
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:01,090
Hi, Sheldon.

78
00:03:01,090 --> 00:03:02,170
Nice sword.

79
00:03:02,170 --> 00:03:03,870
It's part of my sword collection.

80
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:06,370
- Do you have a sword collection?  - No.

81
00:03:06,370 --> 00:03:08,180
I'm not surprised.

82
00:03:09,290 --> 00:03:11,790
Here's the Batman 612

83
00:03:11,790 --> 00:03:14,180
with the Jim Lee alternate cover that you wanted.

84
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:15,990
Awesome. What do I owe you?

85
00:03:15,990 --> 00:03:17,030
40 bucks.

86
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:17,900
Good deal.

87
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:18,900
Sucker.

88
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:21,360
Didn't even ask for the friends and family discount.

89
00:03:22,050 --> 00:03:23,600
Hey, I'm having a party at my house on Friday,

90
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:25,050
and I was hoping you would stop by.

91
00:03:25,050 --> 00:03:26,230
Will there be girls there?

92
00:03:26,230 --> 00:03:27,320
Yeah, of course.

93
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:29,900
'Cause there wasn't last time.

94
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:33,970
There will be girls.

95
00:03:34,390 --> 00:03:36,200
You guys are invited if you want to come by.

96
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:37,440
Thank you.

97
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:38,530
All right, great.

98
00:03:38,530 --> 00:03:39,770
Later.

99
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:44,390
I see what you're doing.

100
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:45,630
You accept an invitation

101
00:03:45,630 --> 00:03:47,820
to a party at the home of my sworn enemy,

102
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:49,560
he tells everyone we're going to be there,

103
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:51,500
and when we don't show, he looks the fool.

104
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:53,310
Fiendishly clever.

105
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:56,600
I was actually thinking about going.

106
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:00,330
And then declaring the party a fiasco and storming out,

107
00:04:00,330 --> 00:04:02,490
leaving him humiliated in front of his guests.

108
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:03,820
Love it.

109
00:04:05,170 --> 00:04:07,570
No, I was gonna grab Raj and Howard

110
00:04:07,570 --> 00:04:09,110
and have a good time.

111
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:10,740
Oh, great, more guys.

112
00:04:11,530 --> 00:04:14,590
It's gonna be another Wil Wheaton sausage-fest.

113
00:04:40,490 --> 00:04:42,250
Sheldon, can you grab me a water?

114
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:44,400
Possibly.

115
00:04:45,010 --> 00:04:46,120
Can you or can't you?

116
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,030
It's not that simple, Leonard.

117
00:04:48,030 --> 00:04:50,090
It never is, is it?

118
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:53,550
At this moment,our relationship exists

119
00:04:53,550 --> 00:04:55,970
in two mutually contradictory states.

120
00:04:55,970 --> 00:04:58,790
Until you either do not go or go

121
00:04:58,790 --> 00:05:00,070
to Wil Wheaton's party,

122
00:05:00,070 --> 00:05:04,080
you are simultaneously my friend and not my friend.

123
00:05:04,820 --> 00:05:06,560
I'm characterizing this phenomenon

124
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:09,280
as Schrodinger's Friendship.

125
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:12,880
Got it. Can I have my water?

126
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:14,370
Of course.

127
00:05:14,370 --> 00:05:16,760
Now get it yourself, you traitor.

128
00:05:19,540 --> 00:05:21,170
Wait, what is going on?

129
00:05:21,170 --> 00:05:22,510
In case you have forgotten,

130
00:05:22,510 --> 00:05:23,920
Schrodinger's cat is a thought experiment...

131
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:25,760
No, no, I didn't forget.

132
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:28,530
Um, there's this cat in a box and until you open it,

133
00:05:28,530 --> 00:05:31,010
it's either dead or alive or both.

134
00:05:31,010 --> 00:05:32,000
Although, back in Nebraska,

135
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,170
our cat got stuck in my brother's camp trunk,

136
00:05:34,170 --> 00:05:35,400
and we did not need to open it

137
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,880
to know there was all kinds of dead cat in there.

138
00:05:39,730 --> 00:05:41,610
Homespun stories, knowledge of physics

139
00:05:41,610 --> 00:05:42,820
and a bosom that defies it.

140
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:45,010
You're the whole package, aren't you?

141
00:05:46,970 --> 00:05:48,180
Sorry I'm late.

142
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:50,290
Uh, I got great news.

143
00:05:50,290 --> 00:05:52,500
NASA picked my team's design

144
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:54,380
for the deep field space telescope

145
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:57,360
that's going on the International Space Station this spring.

146
00:05:58,890 --> 00:06:00,150
Howie, that's wonderful!

147
00:06:00,150 --> 00:06:01,380
Congratulations!

148
00:06:01,380 --> 00:06:02,240
It gets better.

149
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:04,570
Someone has to go up with the telescope

150
00:06:04,570 --> 00:06:06,930
as a payload specialist,

151
00:06:06,930 --> 00:06:09,410
and guess who that someone is.

152
00:06:09,410 --> 00:06:11,430
Muhammad Li.

153
00:06:13,450 --> 00:06:15,290
Who's Muhammad Li?

154
00:06:15,290 --> 00:06:17,890
Muhammad is the most common first name in the world,

155
00:06:17,890 --> 00:06:19,760
Li, the most common surname.

156
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:20,850
As I didn't know the answer,

157
00:06:20,850 --> 00:06:23,460
I thought that gave me a mathematical edge.

158
00:06:24,430 --> 00:06:25,820
It's me, Sheldon.

159
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:26,330
It's me.

160
00:06:26,330 --> 00:06:27,640
I'm going up in space!

161
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:30,420
Technically, I'm an astronaut.

162
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:32,740
That's amazing!

163
00:06:33,350 --> 00:06:34,050
Hang on a second.

164
00:06:34,050 --> 00:06:35,940
NASA doesn't have a shuttle anymore.

165
00:06:35,940 --> 00:06:37,020
How are you going to get up there?

166
00:06:37,020 --> 00:06:38,120
Oh, well, it's really cool.

167
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:40,470
You fly to Moscow, they take you out to Kazakhstan,

168
00:06:40,470 --> 00:06:42,600
and then you get into a Russian Soyuz rocket

169
00:06:42,600 --> 00:06:44,560
which shoots you into a low earth orbit.

170
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:45,980
Or just sits there on the launch pad

171
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:47,110
because the Kazakhi mafia

172
00:06:47,110 --> 00:06:49,550
sold the rocket fuel on the black market.

173
00:06:51,880 --> 00:06:53,920
Are those Russian rockets safe?

174
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:55,760
Well, I mean, safe as it can be when it was built

175
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:58,170
by the good folks who brought you Chernobyl.

176
00:07:00,140 --> 00:07:02,540
I'd like to propose a toast.

177
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:05,570
The dream to go up into space is one we all share,

178
00:07:05,570 --> 00:07:07,680
and Howard's making that a reality.

179
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:09,360
We're all very proud of you.

180
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:10,730
Cheers.

181
00:07:11,890 --> 00:07:13,200
That was a lovely toast.

182
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:14,290
- Kudos.  - Thank you.

183
00:07:14,290 --> 00:07:16,400
Simultaneously, a festival of cloying cliche.

184
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:18,090
You sicken me.

185
00:07:26,650 --> 00:07:28,070
You're really quiet.

186
00:07:28,070 --> 00:07:29,460
Is everything okay?

187
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:31,980
Fine.

188
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:36,290
Just a little tired.

189
00:07:36,290 --> 00:07:38,340
I hope not too tired, because I'm feeling

190
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:41,100
particularly masculine right now.

191
00:07:42,050 --> 00:07:44,980
All systems go, if you catch my drift.

192
00:07:45,570 --> 00:07:48,540
I always catch your drift.

193
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:51,490
All right, well, something's obviously bugging you.

194
00:07:51,490 --> 00:07:53,180
What is it?

195
00:07:53,180 --> 00:07:54,280
I just can't believe you signed up

196
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:57,270
for the space program without even talking to me.

197
00:07:57,270 --> 00:08:00,420
Oh, I get it.

198
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:02,980
You're worried about me.

199
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:04,170
That is so sweet.

200
00:08:04,170 --> 00:08:09,270
You know, there's a saying we have at NASA.

201
00:08:12,020 --> 00:08:14,690
What makes the right stuff so right

202
00:08:14,690 --> 00:08:17,750
is that it always comes home.

203
00:08:19,220 --> 00:08:21,590
Just stop talking, Howard.

204
00:08:23,010 --> 00:08:24,800
This isn't the reaction I expected

205
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:27,800
when I told you I was gonna be an astronaut.

206
00:08:27,800 --> 00:08:29,760
What did you think was going to happen?

207
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:32,290
Honestly? Sex.

208
00:08:34,150 --> 00:08:34,930
Howard.

209
00:08:34,930 --> 00:08:37,290
Do you realize what a big deal this is?

210
00:08:37,290 --> 00:08:41,430
What an honor it is to be chosen to go into space?

211
00:08:41,430 --> 00:08:42,420
Yeah, I get it.

212
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:45,300
I just wish you included me in the decision.

213
00:08:45,300 --> 00:08:47,160
We're supposed to be partners.

214
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:49,270
We're supposed to be a team.

215
00:08:50,810 --> 00:08:53,450
I'm sorry. You're right.

216
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:56,300
Okay, let's try this again.

217
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:59,640
Bernadette, an opportunity has come up

218
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:05,200
that impacts both of us, and I'd like to discuss it.

219
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:08,180
Okay.

220
00:09:08,180 --> 00:09:10,470
I've been offered a chance to go up

221
00:09:10,470 --> 00:09:14,750
to the International Space Station for three weeks.

222
00:09:14,750 --> 00:09:17,220
What are your thoughts on that?

223
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:19,310
Well, first of all, thank you

224
00:09:19,310 --> 00:09:23,000
for including me in the decision-making process.

225
00:09:23,670 --> 00:09:25,720
Hey, we're a team.

226
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:27,990
So, what do you think?

227
00:09:27,990 --> 00:09:29,810
No.

228
00:09:30,530 --> 00:09:31,460
No?

229
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:33,090
No.

230
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:36,400
Why not?

231
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:41,630
Howard, my father was a police officer.

232
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:43,390
We never knew from one night to the next

233
00:09:43,390 --> 00:09:45,540
if he was going to come home alive.

234
00:09:45,540 --> 00:09:46,830
It was horrible.

235
00:09:46,830 --> 00:09:49,130
And I don't want to live that way with you.

236
00:09:52,360 --> 00:09:55,990
Hey, my father abandoned me and my mother

237
00:09:55,990 --> 00:09:57,840
when I was 11.

238
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:00,840
We never saw him again.

239
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:03,780
Oh, boo-hoo, you're not going to space!

240
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:11,230
Pretty cool about Howard, huh?

241
00:10:11,230 --> 00:10:13,160
Don't talk to me as if nothing's happened between us.

242
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:15,410
And yes, it sure is, buddy.

243
00:10:17,930 --> 00:10:20,450
For God's sake, will you stop with the Schrodinger stuff.

244
00:10:20,450 --> 00:10:22,270
Would you prefer a simpler application

245
00:10:22,270 --> 00:10:23,940
of Heisenberg's uncertainty principle,

246
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:24,820
in which I could either

247
00:10:24,820 --> 00:10:26,940
know where you are or whether I like you,

248
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:28,780
but not both.

249
00:10:30,130 --> 00:10:32,630
You never stop talking, do you?

250
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:35,360
I don't understand.

251
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:36,150
What differences does it make

252
00:10:36,150 --> 00:10:37,900
if Leonard goes to Wil Wheaton's party?

253
00:10:37,900 --> 00:10:40,640
Wil Wheaton is Sheldon's mortal enemy.

254
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:42,140
Mortal enemy?

255
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:43,150
Sheldon, I know you're a bit

256
00:10:43,150 --> 00:10:45,400
of a left-handed monkey wrench, but...

257
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:48,650
you really have a mortal enemy?

258
00:10:48,650 --> 00:10:51,220
In fact, I have 61 of them.

259
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:53,540
Would you like to see the list?

260
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:56,080
Oh, say no, say no, say no, say no.

261
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:57,470
You just got off the list.

262
00:10:57,470 --> 00:10:59,430
Would you like back on it?

263
00:11:01,750 --> 00:11:03,100
This'll just take a moment.

264
00:11:03,100 --> 00:11:06,090
It's on a five and a quarter inch floppy.

265
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:13,750
A floppy disk?

266
00:11:13,750 --> 00:11:16,260
Well, I started the list when I was nine.

267
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:18,760
How did Wil Wheaton get on the list?

268
00:11:18,760 --> 00:11:21,010
- Oh, God! - Oh! Come on!

269
00:11:21,010 --> 00:11:23,840
As a child, I loved Wesley Crusher,

270
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:25,780
Wil Wheaton's character on Star Trek.

271
00:11:25,780 --> 00:11:29,480
So, I drove for hours by bus to a Star Trek convention

272
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:31,570
at which Wil Wheaton was scheduled to appear,

273
00:11:31,570 --> 00:11:34,690
so that I could get my Wesley Crusher action figure signed.

274
00:11:34,690 --> 00:11:36,390
But he never showed, because

275
00:11:36,390 --> 00:11:38,700
apparently, it was cooler for him

276
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:42,390
to be the lower-left corner on Hollywood Squares.

277
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:45,630
Oh, damn!

278
00:11:45,630 --> 00:11:47,300
The floppy failed.

279
00:11:47,300 --> 00:11:49,870
Well, whoever was in charge of quality control

280
00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:51,970
at the Verbatim Corporation in 1989,

281
00:11:51,970 --> 00:11:54,880
congratulations, you just made the list.

282
00:12:00,250 --> 00:12:00,820
Howard?

283
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:02,610
Change your mind about sex?

284
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:05,600
I'm still mad, but I'll do it.

285
00:12:07,260 --> 00:12:09,850
No, I've just been thinking.

286
00:12:10,780 --> 00:12:13,560
It doesn't matter if I'm afraid for your safety.

287
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:14,540
I don't want to be the person

288
00:12:14,540 --> 00:12:17,420
who stands between you and your dreams.

289
00:12:17,690 --> 00:12:18,670
Really?

290
00:12:18,670 --> 00:12:19,790
Really.

291
00:12:19,790 --> 00:12:21,630
If going into space means that much to you,

292
00:12:21,630 --> 00:12:24,180
I will never say another word about it.

293
00:12:24,180 --> 00:12:25,900
Thank you.

294
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:27,270
I love you.

295
00:12:27,270 --> 00:12:28,840
I love you, too.

296
00:12:32,340 --> 00:12:34,100
So, sex now?

297
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:36,930
Okay.

298
00:12:36,930 --> 00:12:37,990
I just forgot to brush my teeth.

299
00:12:37,990 --> 00:12:39,630
I'll be right back.

300
00:12:47,230 --> 00:12:50,120
* Oh, baby

301
00:12:56,940 --> 00:12:58,440
* I'm

302
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:00,270
* I'm not in love with you...

303
00:13:00,270 --> 00:13:05,370
Over my dead body my son goes into outer space!

304
00:13:08,650 --> 00:13:11,030
I'm ready.

305
00:13:14,530 --> 00:13:15,750
Wait, let me see if I got this right.

306
00:13:15,750 --> 00:13:16,970
You actually asked Bernadette

307
00:13:16,970 --> 00:13:19,350
to leave your house in the middle of the night?

308
00:13:19,350 --> 00:13:20,240
What choice did I have?

309
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:21,520
She went behind my back

310
00:13:21,520 --> 00:13:23,700
and turned my own mother against me.

311
00:13:23,700 --> 00:13:26,200
Wow. You're not only our first astronaut.

312
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:29,980
You're also the first one of us to kick a girl out of bed.

313
00:13:31,610 --> 00:13:33,980
You're like a rock star.

314
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:36,810
Little bit.

315
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:39,670
I hate to say it, but she did kind of betray you.

316
00:13:39,670 --> 00:13:40,880
Interesting.

317
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:45,610
You see betrayal in others, but not yourself.

318
00:13:46,630 --> 00:13:49,690
Going to Wheaton's party is not betraying you.

319
00:13:49,690 --> 00:13:51,600
Oh, of course you would have to believe that.

320
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:54,560
Evil always thinks it's doing right.

321
00:13:54,910 --> 00:13:56,290
"Excuse me, Stormtrooper. "

322
00:13:56,290 --> 00:13:59,020
"These are the droids you're looking for."

323
00:14:00,470 --> 00:14:02,170
I'm going to a party.

324
00:14:02,170 --> 00:14:06,480
I'm not turning R2-D2 and C-3PO over to the empire!

325
00:14:09,100 --> 00:14:10,790
Not yet.

326
00:14:12,030 --> 00:14:13,140
So, what's gonna happen next?

327
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:15,200
Are you and Bernadette going to break up?

328
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:15,790
I don't know.

329
00:14:15,790 --> 00:14:16,960
If we're going to get back together,

330
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:19,500
she's going to have to apologize and accept  that

331
00:14:19,500 --> 00:14:23,360
I'm a grown man who can make his own decisions.

332
00:14:23,360 --> 00:14:26,040
Then she's going to have to convince your mother to let you go into space.

333
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:27,720
Obviously.

334
00:14:30,730 --> 00:14:32,340
I had no choice.

335
00:14:32,340 --> 00:14:34,130
I had to tell his mother.

336
00:14:34,130 --> 00:14:36,750
He can't go to space.

337
00:14:36,750 --> 00:14:39,180
He's like a baby bird.

338
00:14:41,210 --> 00:14:43,150
Do you know he once got an asthma attack

339
00:14:43,150 --> 00:14:46,030
from reading an old library book?

340
00:14:47,210 --> 00:14:48,200
You're kidding.

341
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:49,940
No, I was there that day.

342
00:14:50,380 --> 00:14:53,560
Sheldon threw his back out handing him that book.

343
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:57,040
I don't know what I'm going to do.

344
00:14:57,040 --> 00:14:58,890
I don't want to break up over this.

345
00:14:58,890 --> 00:15:01,340
Okay, why don't you just tell him you made a mistake?

346
00:15:01,340 --> 00:15:04,130
Do you guys think it was a mistake?

347
00:15:04,130 --> 00:15:06,490
Am I the bad guy in this?

348
00:15:06,490 --> 00:15:08,060
It's not for us to judge.

349
00:15:08,060 --> 00:15:09,970
We're just here to provide comfort and support

350
00:15:09,970 --> 00:15:13,460
while you come to grips with what a despicable thing you've done.

351
00:15:15,910 --> 00:15:17,810
Oh, God, you're right.

352
00:15:17,810 --> 00:15:22,230
I took our love and threw it under his bus-sized mother.

353
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:26,440
I need to apologize.

354
00:15:26,440 --> 00:15:28,190
Well, that-that's good. I'm glad you came to that.

355
00:15:28,190 --> 00:15:32,180
But before you do, let me just ask you a big picture question.

356
00:15:32,180 --> 00:15:33,690
What?

357
00:15:34,170 --> 00:15:36,410
Are you 100% positive

358
00:15:36,410 --> 00:15:39,840
you love and want to marry Howard Wolowitz?

359
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:42,450
I do, with all my heart.

360
00:15:42,450 --> 00:15:44,730
Got it.  Just had to check.

361
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:49,960
He's great.

362
00:15:54,160 --> 00:15:54,980
All right, Sheldon.

363
00:15:54,980 --> 00:15:56,160
We're going to Wil's.

364
00:15:56,160 --> 00:15:57,630
This is your last chance.

365
00:15:57,630 --> 00:16:00,850
No, Leonard, this is your last chance.

366
00:16:01,030 --> 00:16:01,890
One day,

367
00:16:01,890 --> 00:16:04,640
a historian is going to come to you and say,

368
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:07,890
"Is it true you were friends with Dr. Sheldon Cooper?"

369
00:16:07,890 --> 00:16:08,790
And you're going to have to

370
00:16:08,790 --> 00:16:11,880
choke back a hot sob of regret and humiliation

371
00:16:11,880 --> 00:16:13,950
as you mumble, "I was, but

372
00:16:13,950 --> 00:16:15,390
I chose to go to a party

373
00:16:15,390 --> 00:16:19,260
thrown by the one kid from Stand By Me that no one remembers."

374
00:16:23,120 --> 00:16:25,000
- You want to drive? - Sure.

375
00:16:29,890 --> 00:16:30,960
- Hey, Sheldon? - Oh, good.

376
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:32,820
You picked me, you picked me.

377
00:16:33,600 --> 00:16:35,950
No, I just got a text from Stuart.

378
00:16:35,950 --> 00:16:38,310
Brent Spiner is at the party.

379
00:16:38,310 --> 00:16:39,380
Brent Spiner?

380
00:16:39,380 --> 00:16:40,530
Yes.

381
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:43,010
I don't care.

382
00:16:44,490 --> 00:16:45,620
Really?

383
00:16:45,620 --> 00:16:46,540
Brent Spiner,

384
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:48,690
Mr. Data himself. You love him.

385
00:16:48,690 --> 00:16:53,010
I did, but I think I've kind of outgrown Star Trek.

386
00:16:55,660 --> 00:16:58,230
You know, stock characters,

387
00:16:58,230 --> 00:17:00,080
ludicrous plots, "Beam me up."

388
00:17:00,080 --> 00:17:02,070
What a load of hooey.

389
00:17:02,990 --> 00:17:04,170
I'm going.

390
00:17:04,170 --> 00:17:05,740
Live long and prosper, Sheldon.

391
00:17:05,740 --> 00:17:08,120
Yeah, even that. You look like a dork.

392
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:14,830
Howard, Bernadette's here!

393
00:17:14,830 --> 00:17:16,230
Tell her I'm not home!

394
00:17:16,230 --> 00:17:18,100
What kind of a schmuck play is that?!

395
00:17:18,100 --> 00:17:20,290
She can hear you shouting!

396
00:17:23,250 --> 00:17:25,020
Can we talk?

397
00:17:25,020 --> 00:17:27,810
You can. I have nothing to say.

398
00:17:28,380 --> 00:17:29,740
All right.

399
00:17:30,390 --> 00:17:31,310
I just wanted to tell you

400
00:17:31,310 --> 00:17:33,470
I'm sorry I said something to your mother.

401
00:17:33,470 --> 00:17:35,890
I was gonna tell her eventually,

402
00:17:35,890 --> 00:17:38,420
but you went behind my back.

403
00:17:38,420 --> 00:17:40,070
I know. I'm sorry.

404
00:17:40,070 --> 00:17:41,740
I got scared.

405
00:17:41,740 --> 00:17:42,660
If you're gonna love me,

406
00:17:42,660 --> 00:17:44,970
you're gonna have to love the whole package:

407
00:17:44,970 --> 00:17:49,830
the tenderhearted poet and the crazy daredevil.

408
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:52,400
I know.

409
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:54,010
Well, don't say it if you're not gonna mean it,

410
00:17:54,010 --> 00:17:56,620
'cause I'm not just gonna stop with the space station.

411
00:17:56,620 --> 00:17:59,970
Yeah, I want to go to the Moon, I want to go to Mars.

412
00:17:59,970 --> 00:18:04,340
I want to take a one-man sub to the lowest depths of the ocean.

413
00:18:04,340 --> 00:18:05,440
Really?

414
00:18:05,440 --> 00:18:08,880
You got seasick on Pirates of the Caribbean.

415
00:18:10,620 --> 00:18:14,290
Well, those big kids were rocking it.

416
00:18:15,190 --> 00:18:18,290
I just did what I did because I love you so much,

417
00:18:18,290 --> 00:18:21,800
and the thought of losing you is more than I can handle.

418
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:23,420
Really?

419
00:18:23,420 --> 00:18:26,090
You're my soul mate.

420
00:18:27,670 --> 00:18:29,350
This is where you kiss me.

421
00:18:29,350 --> 00:18:31,270
Right, right.

422
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:35,690
Make up all you want!

423
00:18:35,690 --> 00:18:40,320
Your tuchis is not leaving this planet!

424
00:18:44,920 --> 00:18:47,150
Hey, you know that beautiful actress who plays

425
00:18:47,150 --> 00:18:49,300
the Borg Queen in First Contact?

426
00:18:49,300 --> 00:18:53,360
- Yeah. - Well, I just met her gynecologist!

427
00:18:56,420 --> 00:18:57,730
What are you doing here?

428
00:18:57,730 --> 00:18:59,590
Fighting for our friendship.

429
00:18:59,590 --> 00:19:01,690
As peculiar and annoying as you can be,

430
00:19:01,690 --> 00:19:03,720
you're still my little buddy.

431
00:19:03,720 --> 00:19:05,550
I'm not going to let that end here tonight.

432
00:19:05,550 --> 00:19:09,220
Now put down that drink, let's meet Brent Spiner and go home.

433
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:12,140
Hey, Sheldon, I'm so glad you made it.

434
00:19:12,140 --> 00:19:14,450
I found something I think you might like.

435
00:19:14,880 --> 00:19:17,380
What I'd like is for him to have a more depressing home.

436
00:19:17,380 --> 00:19:19,260
This is quite lovely.

437
00:19:20,130 --> 00:19:21,850
This is for you.

438
00:19:22,040 --> 00:19:26,040
An original mint-in-package Wesley Crusher action figure.

439
00:19:26,040 --> 00:19:27,590
I remembered your story about the time you went

440
00:19:27,590 --> 00:19:28,880
to a convention when you were a kid

441
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:31,090
to get one signed, and I didn't show up.

442
00:19:31,090 --> 00:19:32,820
Look at what I wrote.

443
00:19:33,370 --> 00:19:36,690
"To Sheldon, sorry this took so long.

444
00:19:36,690 --> 00:19:39,090
Your friend, Wil Wheaton."

445
00:19:39,090 --> 00:19:40,380
It's my last one.

446
00:19:40,380 --> 00:19:42,670
I want you to have it.

447
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:47,330
Look, everyone. Wil Wheaton is my friend!

448
00:19:52,290 --> 00:19:55,180
I haven't seen one of these in years.

449
00:19:56,690 --> 00:19:58,060
Remember how we used to make these things look

450
00:19:58,060 --> 00:20:00,300
like they were masturbating?

451
00:20:01,500 --> 00:20:03,450
Brent Spiner, what have you done?!

452
00:20:03,450 --> 00:20:04,740
That was an original mint-in-package

453
00:20:04,740 --> 00:20:06,320
Wesley Crusher action figure

454
00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:08,940
signed by my close personal friend, Wil Wheaton.

455
00:20:08,940 --> 00:20:10,260
Sorry, Slim.

456
00:20:10,260 --> 00:20:12,680
I've got some Mr. Data dolls in the trunk of my car.

457
00:20:12,680 --> 00:20:14,160
You want me to sign one for you?

458
00:20:14,160 --> 00:20:16,430
You've already signed something, Brent Spiner.

459
00:20:16,430 --> 00:20:18,940
Your name on my list.

460
00:20:21,090 --> 00:20:24,470
From this moment on, you are my mortal enemy.

461
00:20:24,470 --> 00:20:27,630
Don't worry. It doesn't take up a whole lot of your time.

462
00:20:28,310 --> 00:20:29,290
Come on, buddy.

463
00:20:29,290 --> 00:20:32,050
Let's not waste another second on this loser.

464
00:20:32,050 --> 00:20:34,470
Love your house.

465
00:20:35,380 --> 00:20:37,460
Can we get autographed dolls?

466
00:20:37,460 --> 00:20:39,260
Sure.

467
00:20:39,260 --> 00:20:41,840
20 bucks.

468
00:20:43,730 --> 00:20:45,240
10.

469
00:20:48,120 --> 00:20:49,210
18.

470
00:20:49,210 --> 00:20:49,970
12.

471
00:20:49,970 --> 00:20:50,890
16.

472
00:20:50,890 --> 00:20:52,190
Two for $30.

473
00:20:52,190 --> 00:20:55,120
And you come to my birthday party.

474
00:20:57,850 --> 00:20:59,440
Done.

